[[:db/cas 17592186672200 :question/worked-solution "Separate and integrate: $\\int dR/(R^2+16)=ax+C_1$, so $\\frac14\\arctan(R/4)=ax+C_1$. Multiply by 4 and absorb 4C₁ into C, then take tangent. Each solution is restricted to an interval avoiding the poles of tan. Differentiation gives $R\\prime=16a\\sec^2(4ax+C)=a(R^2+16)$." "Separate and integrate: $$\n\\displaystyle \\int dR/(R^2+16)=ax+C_1,\n$$ so $\\frac14\\arctan(R/4)=ax+C_1$. Multiply by 4 and absorb 4C₁ into C, then take tangent. Each solution is restricted to an interval avoiding the poles of tan. Differentiation gives $R\\prime=16a\\sec^2(4ax+C)=a(R^2+16)$."] [:db/cas 17592186672221 :question/worked-solution "Integration by parts gives $\\int -xe^x dx=(1-x)e^x+C$. The condition at zero gives $C=1$. Differentiate to check $y\\prime=-xe^x$ and substitute zero to check $y(0)=2$." "Integration by parts gives $$\n\\displaystyle \\int -xe^x dx=(1-x)e^x+C.\n$$ The condition at zero gives $C=1$. Differentiate to check $y\\prime=-xe^x$ and substitute zero to check $y(0)=2$."] [:db/cas 17592186672232 :question/problem "**(d)** Use part (b) to show that $\\displaystyle-\\nabla\\phi\\cdot\\mathbf r\\prime=m\\mathbf v\\cdot\\frac{d\\mathbf v}{dt}$. Conclude from parts (a) and (c) that $\\displaystyle-\\frac{d\\phi}{dt}=\\frac m2\\frac{d}{dt}|\\mathbf v|^2$.\n\nYour response: {{response}}" "**(d)** Use part (b) to show that $$\n\\displaystyle-\\nabla\\phi\\cdot\\mathbf r\\prime=m\\mathbf v\\cdot\\frac{d\\mathbf v}{dt}.\n$$ Conclude from parts (a) and (c) that $$\n\\displaystyle-\\frac{d\\phi}{dt}=\\frac m2\\frac{d}{dt}|\\mathbf v|^2.\n$$\n\nYour response: {{response}}"] [:db/cas 17592186672247 :question/problem "**(b)** Evaluate $\\int_{AB}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf T\\,ds$. This equals $\\phi(B)-\\phi(A)$.\n\nIntegral: {{answer}}." "**(b)** Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{AB}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf T\\,ds.\n$$ This equals $\\phi(B)-\\phi(A)$.\n\nIntegral: {{answer}}."] [:db/cas 17592186672273 :question/worked-solution "For $x\\ne0$, $\\int2/x^3dx=-1/x^2$, so the integrating factor is $e^{-1/x^2}$. Then $(e^{-1/x^2}y)\\prime=3$, giving $y=e^{1/x^2}(3x+C)$ on an interval avoiding zero. The supplied key has a coefficient typo in one intermediate line; the original equation and its final answer agree." "For $x\\ne0$, $$\n\\displaystyle \\int2/x^3dx=-1/x^2,\n$$ so the integrating factor is $e^{-1/x^2}$. Then $(e^{-1/x^2}y)\\prime=3$, giving $y=e^{1/x^2}(3x+C)$ on an interval avoiding zero. The supplied key has a coefficient typo in one intermediate line; the original equation and its final answer agree."] [:db/cas 17592186672286 :question/problem "**(d)** $\\displaystyle\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf T\\,ds=0$ for $\\mathbf F=\\langle-y,x\\rangle$ and the counterclockwise circle of radius $2$ centered at $(1,0)$.\n\nYour response: {{response}}" "**(d)** $$\n\\displaystyle\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf T\\,ds=0\n$$ for $\\mathbf F=\\langle-y,x\\rangle$ and the counterclockwise circle of radius $2$ centered at $(1,0)$.\n\nYour response: {{response}}"] [:db/cas 17592186672305 :question/problem "**(c)** Evaluate $\\displaystyle\\oint_C e^{-x}\\cos y\\,dx+e^{-x}\\sin y\\,dy$, where $C$ is the counterclockwise rectangle with vertices $(\\pm2,\\pm1)$.\n\nIntegral: {{answer}}." "**(c)** Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle\\oint_C e^{-x}\\cos y\\,dx+e^{-x}\\sin y\\,dy,\n$$ where $C$ is the counterclockwise rectangle with vertices $(\\pm2,\\pm1)$.\n\nIntegral: {{answer}}."] [:db/cas 17592186672348 :question/problem "**2.** For the same constants and initial population, find $\\displaystyle\\lim_{t\\to\\infty}P(t)$: {{limit}}." "**2.** For the same constants and initial population, find $$\n\\displaystyle\\lim_{t\\to\\infty}P(t):\n$$ {{limit}}."] [:db/cas 17592186672360 :question/problem "Source question: Problem 2, PDF page 3.\n\nLet\n\n$$\\mathbf F=\\left\\langle\\frac{x^3}{x^2+1},\\sin^4 y,\\ln(z^2+1)\\right\\rangle,$$\n\nand $C:\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle\\sin t,\\cos t,\\sin t\\rangle$, $0\\le t\\le2\\pi$. Evaluate $\\displaystyle\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf T\\,ds$.\n\nIntegral: {{answer}}." "Source question: Problem 2, PDF page 3.\n\nLet\n\n$$\\mathbf F=\\left\\langle\\frac{x^3}{x^2+1},\\sin^4 y,\\ln(z^2+1)\\right\\rangle,$$\n\nand $C:\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle\\sin t,\\cos t,\\sin t\\rangle$, $0\\le t\\le2\\pi$. Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf T\\,ds.\n$$\n\nIntegral: {{answer}}."] [:db/cas 17592186672360 :question/worked-solution "Every component depends only on its corresponding coordinate and is continuous for all real inputs. A global potential is $\\Phi=\\int_0^x u^3/(u^2+1)\\,du+\\int_0^y\\sin^4v\\,dv+\\int_0^z\\ln(w^2+1)\\,dw$. Differentiating these one-variable integrals gives $\\nabla\\Phi=\\mathbf F$. Since the curve begins and ends at $(0,1,0)$, the integral is zero. There is no need to evaluate the three antiderivatives explicitly." "Every component depends only on its corresponding coordinate and is continuous for all real inputs. A global potential is $$\n\\displaystyle \\Phi=\\int_0^x u^3/(u^2+1)\\,du+\\int_0^y\\sin^4v\\,dv+\\int_0^z\\ln(w^2+1)\\,dw.\n$$ Differentiating these one-variable integrals gives $\\nabla\\Phi=\\mathbf F$. Since the curve begins and ends at $(0,1,0)$, the integral is zero. There is no need to evaluate the three antiderivatives explicitly."] [:db/cas 17592186672372 :question/problem "**(c)** $\\displaystyle\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf T\\,ds=0$ for $\\mathbf F=\\langle x,-y\\rangle$ and the counterclockwise circle of radius $2$ centered at $(1,0)$.\n\nYour response: {{response}}" "**(c)** $$\n\\displaystyle\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf T\\,ds=0\n$$ for $\\mathbf F=\\langle x,-y\\rangle$ and the counterclockwise circle of radius $2$ centered at $(1,0)$.\n\nYour response: {{response}}"] [:db/cas 17592186672403 :question/problem "**(a)** Evaluate $\\int_{OA}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf T\\,ds$. This equals $\\phi(A)$.\n\nIntegral: {{answer}}." "**(a)** Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{OA}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf T\\,ds.\n$$ This equals $\\phi(A)$.\n\nIntegral: {{answer}}."] [:db/cas 17592186672770 :question/problem "An aquarium initially holds $250L$ of water containing $6.25kg$ of dissolved minerals.\nA solution containing $0.025kg/L$ of dissolved minerals is pumped into the aquarium at a rate of $20L/min$, while the completely mixed water is drained out at a rate of $30L/min$. If the amount of minerals $Q(t)$, in kilograms, in the tank $t$ minutes after pumping begins is given by\n$Q(t) = α(1 - \\frac{t}{25}) + β(1 - \\frac{t}{25})^{3}$\nwhere $α$ and $β$ are constants, what is the value of $α + β$?\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." "An aquarium initially holds $250L$ of water containing $6.25kg$ of dissolved minerals.\nA solution containing $0.025kg/L$ of dissolved minerals is pumped into the aquarium at a rate of $20L/min$, while the completely mixed water is drained out at a rate of $30L/min$. If the amount of minerals $Q(t)$, in kilograms, in the tank $t$ minutes after pumping begins is given by\n$$\n\\displaystyle Q(t) = α(1 - \\frac{t}{25}) + β(1 - \\frac{t}{25})^{3}\n$$\nwhere $α$ and $β$ are constants, what is the value of $α + β$?\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."] [:db/cas 17592186672774 :question/problem "An aquarium initially holds $600L$ of water containing $49kg$ of dissolved minerals.\nA solution containing $0.035kg/L$ of dissolved minerals is pumped into the aquarium at a rate of $12L/min$, while the completely mixed water is drained out at a rate of $18L/min$. If the amount of minerals $Q(t)$, in kilograms, in the tank $t$ minutes after pumping begins is given by\n$Q(t) = α(1 - \\frac{t}{100}) + β(1 - \\frac{t}{100})^{3}$\nwhere $α$ and $β$ are constants, what is the value of $α + β$?\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." "An aquarium initially holds $600L$ of water containing $49kg$ of dissolved minerals.\nA solution containing $0.035kg/L$ of dissolved minerals is pumped into the aquarium at a rate of $12L/min$, while the completely mixed water is drained out at a rate of $18L/min$. If the amount of minerals $Q(t)$, in kilograms, in the tank $t$ minutes after pumping begins is given by\n$$\n\\displaystyle Q(t) = α(1 - \\frac{t}{100}) + β(1 - \\frac{t}{100})^{3}\n$$\nwhere $α$ and $β$ are constants, what is the value of $α + β$?\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."] [:db/cas 17592186672780 :question/problem "An aquarium initially holds $216L$ of water containing $2.7kg$ of dissolved minerals.\nA solution containing $0.03kg/L$ of dissolved minerals is pumped into the aquarium at a rate of $24L/min$, while the completely mixed water is drained out at a rate of $36L/min$. If the amount of minerals $Q(t)$, in kilograms, in the tank $t$ minutes after pumping begins is given by\n$Q(t) = α(1 - \\frac{t}{18}) + β(1 - \\frac{t}{18})^{3}$\nwhere $α$ and $β$ are constants, what is the value of $α + β$?\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." "An aquarium initially holds $216L$ of water containing $2.7kg$ of dissolved minerals.\nA solution containing $0.03kg/L$ of dissolved minerals is pumped into the aquarium at a rate of $24L/min$, while the completely mixed water is drained out at a rate of $36L/min$. If the amount of minerals $Q(t)$, in kilograms, in the tank $t$ minutes after pumping begins is given by\n$$\n\\displaystyle Q(t) = α(1 - \\frac{t}{18}) + β(1 - \\frac{t}{18})^{3}\n$$\nwhere $α$ and $β$ are constants, what is the value of $α + β$?\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."] [:db/cas 17592186672785 :question/problem "An aquarium initially holds $840L$ of water containing $33.6kg$ of dissolved minerals.\nA solution containing $0.04kg/L$ of dissolved minerals is pumped into the aquarium at a rate of $24L/min$, while the completely mixed water is drained out at a rate of $36L/min$. If the amount of minerals $Q(t)$, in kilograms, in the tank $t$ minutes after pumping begins is given by\n$Q(t) = α(1 - \\frac{t}{70}) + β(1 - \\frac{t}{70})^{3}$\nwhere $α$ and $β$ are constants, what is the value of $α + β$?\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." "An aquarium initially holds $840L$ of water containing $33.6kg$ of dissolved minerals.\nA solution containing $0.04kg/L$ of dissolved minerals is pumped into the aquarium at a rate of $24L/min$, while the completely mixed water is drained out at a rate of $36L/min$. If the amount of minerals $Q(t)$, in kilograms, in the tank $t$ minutes after pumping begins is given by\n$$\n\\displaystyle Q(t) = α(1 - \\frac{t}{70}) + β(1 - \\frac{t}{70})^{3}\n$$\nwhere $α$ and $β$ are constants, what is the value of $α + β$?\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."] [:db/cas 17592186672799 :question/problem "An aquarium initially holds $500L$ of water containing $34kg$ of dissolved minerals.\nA solution containing $0.02kg/L$ of dissolved minerals is pumped into the aquarium at a rate of $20L/min$, while the completely mixed water is drained out at a rate of $30L/min$. If the amount of minerals $Q(t)$, in kilograms, in the tank $t$ minutes after pumping begins is given by\n$Q(t) = α(1 - \\frac{t}{50}) + β(1 - \\frac{t}{50})^{3}$\nwhere $α$ and $β$ are constants, what is the value of $α + β$?\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." "An aquarium initially holds $500L$ of water containing $34kg$ of dissolved minerals.\nA solution containing $0.02kg/L$ of dissolved minerals is pumped into the aquarium at a rate of $20L/min$, while the completely mixed water is drained out at a rate of $30L/min$. If the amount of minerals $Q(t)$, in kilograms, in the tank $t$ minutes after pumping begins is given by\n$$\n\\displaystyle Q(t) = α(1 - \\frac{t}{50}) + β(1 - \\frac{t}{50})^{3}\n$$\nwhere $α$ and $β$ are constants, what is the value of $α + β$?\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."] [:db/cas 17592186673413 :question/worked-solution "Since $A$ is a $8\\times 8$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible\n\n$\\,⇔\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\text{Col}(A) &={R}^{8} \\\\ \\text{dim(Col}(A)) &=8=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\ \\text{Null}(A) &=\\{0\\} \\\\ \\text{dim(Null}(A)) &=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\\right.$\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A)=n.\n$$\n\nThe 3 pivot columns give $\\text{rank}(A)=3.$\n\nBy the rank-nullity theorem,\n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A)=n.\n$$\n\n$$\n3+\\text{nullity}(A)=8,\\qquad \\text{nullity}(A)=8-3=5.\n$$\n\nHence $\\text{rank}(A)=3$ and $\\text{nullity}(A)=5$. Since rank is less than the number of columns, $A$ is not invertible and $\\det(A)=0$. Also, $\\text{Col}(A)$ has dimension 3. The number of pivot columns is 3, and the number of non-pivot columns is 5.\n\n- Statement I is false. $\\text{rank}(A)=3$.\n\n- Statement II is true. The rank is less than the number of columns.\n\n- Statement III is false. $\\text{nullity}(A)=5$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is II only." "Since $A$ is a $8\\times 8$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\,⇔\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\text{Col}(A) &={R}^{8} \\\\ \\text{dim(Col}(A)) &=8=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\ \\text{Null}(A) &=\\{0\\} \\\\ \\text{dim(Null}(A)) &=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A)=n.\n$$\n\nThe 3 pivot columns give $\\text{rank}(A)=3.$\n\nBy the rank-nullity theorem,\n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A)=n.\n$$\n\n$$\n3+\\text{nullity}(A)=8,\\qquad \\text{nullity}(A)=8-3=5.\n$$\n\nHence $\\text{rank}(A)=3$ and $\\text{nullity}(A)=5$. Since rank is less than the number of columns, $A$ is not invertible and $\\det(A)=0$. Also, $\\text{Col}(A)$ has dimension 3. The number of pivot columns is 3, and the number of non-pivot columns is 5.\n\n- Statement I is false. $\\text{rank}(A)=3$.\n\n- Statement II is true. The rank is less than the number of columns.\n\n- Statement III is false. $\\text{nullity}(A)=5$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is II only."] [:db/cas 17592186674390 :question/problem "Find the entry $b_{25}$ of the matrix $B$, where\n$B = \\begin{bmatrix}13 & \\sqrt{2} & 9 & 7 \\\\ 12 & -9 & -6 & -5 \\\\ 5 & 4 & 9 & \\sqrt{3} \\\\ 7 & 8 & -4 & 5\\end{bmatrix}$." "Find the entry $b_{25}$ of the matrix $B$, where\n$$\n\\displaystyle B = \\begin{bmatrix}13 & \\sqrt{2} & 9 & 7 \\\\ 12 & -9 & -6 & -5 \\\\ 5 & 4 & 9 & \\sqrt{3} \\\\ 7 & 8 & -4 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186674400 :question/problem "Find the entry $b_{22}$ of the matrix $B$, where\n$B = \\begin{bmatrix}13 & \\sqrt{2} & 9 & 7 \\\\ 12 & -9 & -6 & -5 \\\\ 5 & 4 & 9 & \\sqrt{3} \\\\ 7 & 8 & -4 & 5\\end{bmatrix}$." "Find the entry $b_{22}$ of the matrix $B$, where\n$$\n\\displaystyle B = \\begin{bmatrix}13 & \\sqrt{2} & 9 & 7 \\\\ 12 & -9 & -6 & -5 \\\\ 5 & 4 & 9 & \\sqrt{3} \\\\ 7 & 8 & -4 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186674400 :question/worked-solution "The entry $b_{22}$ lies at the intersection of the $2$nd row and $2$nd column. Therefore, $b_{22}=-9.$\n\n$B = \\begin{bmatrix}13 & \\sqrt{2} & 9 & 7 \\\\ 12 & -9 & -6 & -5 \\\\ 5 & 4 & 9 & \\sqrt{3} \\\\ 7 & 8 & -4 & 5\\end{bmatrix}$." "The entry $b_{22}$ lies at the intersection of the $2$nd row and $2$nd column. Therefore, $b_{22}=-9.$\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle B = \\begin{bmatrix}13 & \\sqrt{2} & 9 & 7 \\\\ 12 & -9 & -6 & -5 \\\\ 5 & 4 & 9 & \\sqrt{3} \\\\ 7 & 8 & -4 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186674489 :question/problem "Consider the highlighted entry of the matrix $Y$ given below. Describe it in terms of $y_{ij}$.\n\n$Y = \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 9 & -9 & -6 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 4 & 8 \\\\ -8 & 2 & \\boxed{4} & -6 \\\\ -3 & 4 & 8 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$" "Consider the highlighted entry of the matrix $Y$ given below. Describe it in terms of $y_{ij}$.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle Y = \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 9 & -9 & -6 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 4 & 8 \\\\ -8 & 2 & \\boxed{4} & -6 \\\\ -3 & 4 & 8 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186674496 :question/problem "Consider the highlighted entry of the matrix $Y$ given below. Describe it in terms of $y_{ij}$.\n\n$Y = \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 9 & -9 & -6 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 4 & 8 \\\\ -8 & \\boxed{2} & 4 & -6 \\\\ -3 & 4 & 8 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$" "Consider the highlighted entry of the matrix $Y$ given below. Describe it in terms of $y_{ij}$.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle Y = \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 9 & -9 & -6 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 4 & 8 \\\\ -8 & \\boxed{2} & 4 & -6 \\\\ -3 & 4 & 8 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186674498 :question/problem "Consider the highlighted entry of the matrix $P$ given below. Describe it in terms of $p_{ij}$.\n\n$P = \\begin{bmatrix}-7 & 8 & 3 & 3 \\\\ -1 & -2 & -9 & -8 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 5 & 3 \\\\ -6 & -9 & 7 & \\boxed{2}\\end{bmatrix}$" "Consider the highlighted entry of the matrix $P$ given below. Describe it in terms of $p_{ij}$.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle P = \\begin{bmatrix}-7 & 8 & 3 & 3 \\\\ -1 & -2 & -9 & -8 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 5 & 3 \\\\ -6 & -9 & 7 & \\boxed{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186674501 :question/problem "Consider the highlighted entry of the matrix $P$ given below. Describe it in terms of $p_{ij}$.\n\n$P = \\begin{bmatrix}-7 & 8 & 3 & 3 \\\\ -1 & -2 & -9 & -8 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 5 & 3 \\\\ -6 & -9 & \\boxed{7} & 2\\end{bmatrix}$" "Consider the highlighted entry of the matrix $P$ given below. Describe it in terms of $p_{ij}$.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle P = \\begin{bmatrix}-7 & 8 & 3 & 3 \\\\ -1 & -2 & -9 & -8 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 5 & 3 \\\\ -6 & -9 & \\boxed{7} & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"]]
